.SH DESCRIPTION
Run I/O benchmarks against NVMe devices
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B
\fBxnvme-perf-storage-run\fP(1)
Run a time-bounded async IO benchmark against one or more NVMe devices.
Devices are distributed across CPU threads pinned by \fB--cpumask\fP or \fB--cpulist\fP.
With \fB--be\fP 'io_uring_dmabuf' the IO buffers are allocated on the GPU given by
\fB--gpu-id\fP and exported as a dma-buf for the kernel to DMA into.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
  xnvme-perf-storage-verify(1)  For each device: writes --count sequential IOs with a per-LBA pattern,
.fam T
.fi
then reads them back and compares against the expected data.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
  xnvme-perf-storage-cuda-run(1)  Run a time-bounded GPU NVMe I/O benchmark against one or more devices.
.fam T
.fi
All devices run in parallel: one CUDA block per queue, all queues launched
in a single kernel. All devices must use the same LBA size.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
  xnvme-perf-storage-cuda-verify(1)  Write an LBA-stamped pattern to each device through GPU queues and read
.fam T
.fi
it back, verifying that the data matches. Uses the same queue topology
as cuda-run so results are directly comparable.
.RE
.PP

